    {
      "id": "p1ywurw",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "PHYSICAL QUANTITY"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Physics studies matter, energy, motion, force, heat, light, and sound to explain natural phenomena.",
        "A physical quantity requires both a numerical value and a unit to have physical meaning.",
        "There are 7 fundamental quantities in the SI system, which was adopted in 1960.",
        "Derived quantities (like force, speed, and density) are mathematically obtained from fundamental quantities.",
        "Dimensional formulas represent relationships of physical quantities using Mass (M), Length (L), and Time (T)."
      ],
      "id": "2jrqelh"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "A physical quantity is any quantity that can be measured and expressed in numerical value with a unit.",
      "id": "ks1x9dh"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Example:",
      "id": "3d371h8"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Length = 10 m Mass = 5 kg Time = 20 s",
      "id": "s1dsp2y"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Without unit, a number has no physical meaning.",
      "id": "3bjfafq"
    },
    {
      "id": "20c3b50",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "TYPES OF PHYSICAL QUANTITIES"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Physical quantities are of two types:",
      "id": "5ykk325"
    },
    {
      "id": "tc4354g",
      "type": "numberedList",
      "items": [
        "Fundamental Quantities",
        "Derived Quantities"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "hka8byz",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "FUNDAMENTAL QUANTITIES"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<img alt=\"Measurements\" src=\"/books/Precis/Cluster_014_SSC-GK-Physics/images/image_ozhft7d.png\"/>Fundamental quantities are basic quantities which cannot be derived from other quantities.",
      "id": "eksth3o"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "There are seven fundamental quantities in SI system.",
      "id": "izi3tft"
    },
    {
      "id": "kx660tm",
      "type": "numberedList",
      "items": [
        "Length – Meter (m)",
        "Mass – Kilogram (kg)",
        "Time – Second (s)",
        "Temperature – Kelvin (K)",
        "Electric Current – Ampere (A)",
        "Luminous Intensity – Candela (cd)",
        "Amount of Substance – Mole (mol)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Trick to remember:",
      "id": "devvb1v"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "\"My Teacher Likes Playing Chess Daily Morning\"",
      "id": "5wsptu2"
    },
    {
      "id": "z6nwfi2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "DERIVED QUANTITIES"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Derived quantities are obtained from fundamental quantities. Examples:",
      "id": "tirz3cc"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "Speed = Distance / Time",
      "id": "a506te6"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Without unit, a number has no physical meaning.",
      "id": "sv9x8nq"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Acceleration = Change in velocity / Time Force = Mass × Acceleration",
      "id": "ije9z0y"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Work = Force × Distance Power = Work / Time Density = Mass / Volume",
      "id": "f1mkfqr"
    },
    {
      "id": "r5mr9i8",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "SI SYSTEM OF UNITS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "SI stands for International System of Units.",
      "id": "ixsifhf"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "It is accepted globally and used in scientific measurements. It was adopted in the year 1960.",
      "id": "vvqdsw8"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Important SI Units: Length – Meter Mass – Kilogram Time – Second Force – Newton Energy – Joule",
      "id": "bzse72i"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "Pressure – Pascal",
      "id": "7wsoeo8"
    },
    {
      "id": "0fduz35",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "CONVERSION OF UNITS"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "1 kilometer = 1000 meters",
      "id": "b2mqkzz"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "1 meter = 100 centimeters",
      "id": "9teoe4y"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "1 kilogram = 1000 grams",
      "id": "keiedjq"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "1 hour = 3600 seconds",
      "id": "a0kgd9a"
    },
